Metadata: {"id": "Y55", "fqn": "GourResource.gsl_y55", "source": {"gour": {"ref": "Thm 11.4", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.05474v2#nameddest=theorem.11.4", "page": 504}, "hy": [{"ref": "Thm 4", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2408.02722v3#Thmtheorem4"}, {"ref": "Lemma 5", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2408.02722v3#Thmtheorem5"}]}, "statement": "Under the same assumptions, there is a finite nonnegative real number $r=D^{\\mathrm{reg}}(\\rho\\Vert F)$ such that the relative entropy rate $u_n$ and, for every fixed $\\epsilon\\in(0,1)$, the composite testing rate $b_n^\\epsilon$ both converge to $r$. The number $r$ does not depend on $\\epsilon$.", "book": "Common limit of the relative entropy rate and the composite testing rate [corresponds to H–Y Theorem 4, with the tensor power supplied]", "original": {"gour": "\\begin{myt}{\\color{yellow} The Generalized Quantum Stein's Lemma}\n\\begin{theorem}\\label{gsl}\nLet $\\mf$ be a QRT whose sets of free states satisfy the properties above.\nThen, for all $\\eps\\in(0,1)$ and all $\\rho\\in\\md(A)$\n\\be\\label{1158}\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{n}D_{\\min}^{\\eps}\\left(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\big\\|\\mf\\right)=D^{\\reg}(\\rho\\|\\mf)\\;.\n\\ee\n\\end{theorem}", "hy": "\\begin{theorem}[Generalized quantum Stein's lemma]\\Label{TH1}\nFor any $\\epsilon\\in(0,1)$ and any sequence $\\qty{\\mathcal{S}^{(n)}}_n$ of sets of states satisfying conditions (a), (b), and (c), it holds that\n\\begin{align}\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty} -\\frac{1}{n}\\log \\beta_\\epsilon\\left(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\middle\\| \\mathcal{S}^{(n)}\\right)=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{n}\\min_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}^{(n)}}D\\left(\\rho\\middle\\|\\sigma\\right).   \n\\Label{ZXI}\n\\end{align}\n\\end{theorem}\n\nTo prove Theorem~\\ref{TH1}, due to Lemma~\\ref{L1}, it suffices to deal with $\\max_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}_2^{(n)}} \\beta_{\\epsilon}\\left(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\middle\\| \\sigma\\right)$ and $\\min_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}^{(n)}}D\\left(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\middle\\|\\sigma\\right)$; in particular, our strategy focuses on analyzing the following relation instead of \\eqref{ZXI}:\n\\begin{align}\n&\\lim_{n\\to \\infty} -\\frac{1}{n}\\log \n\\max_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}^{(n)}}\n\\beta_\\epsilon\\left(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\middle\\| \\sigma\\right)\n=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{n}\\min_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}^{(n) }}D\\left(\\rho\\middle\\|\\sigma\\right).\n\\Label{XBI}\n\\end{align}\nBefore proceeding with our proof of~\\eqref{XBI}, we note that the existence of limits in~\\eqref{XBI} is not assumed a priori; however, as shown in Section \\ref{S4}, we see that due to Fekete's subadditive lemma~\\cite[Lemma~A.1]{hayashi2016quantum}, the limit $\\lim_{n\\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{n}\\min_{\n\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}^{(n)}\n}D\\left(\\rho\\middle\\|\\sigma\\right)$ on the right-hand side of~\\eqref{XBI} exists, as shown in the following lemma.\nThen, our analysis will bound $\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}-\\frac{1}{n}\\log \n\\max_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}^{(n)}}\n\\beta_\\epsilon\\left(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\middle\\| \\sigma\\right)$ and $\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}-\\frac{1}{n}\\log \n\\max_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}^{(n)}}\n\\beta_\\epsilon\\left(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\middle\\| \\sigma\\right)$ using this limit.\nWe also remark that the convexity of $\\mathcal{S}^{(n)}$ is unnecessary for this lemma.\n\n\\begin{lemma}\\Label{L4}\nFor any sequence $\\qty{\\mathcal{S}^{(n)}}_n$ of sets satisfying conditions (a), (b), and (c) (except for the convexity of $\\mathcal{S}^{(n)}$),\nthe limit\n\\begin{align}\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{n}\\min_{\n\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}^{(n)}\n} D\\left(\\rho\\middle\\|\\sigma\\right)\n\\end{align}\nexists.\n\\end{lemma}"}, "signature_sha256": "339edcc1356ab56c5bb030c82d455b24a090b5c2c05db94f423d45cb848b8ba6", "display": {"section": "hy", "statement": "Under the same assumptions, for every fixed $\\epsilon\\in(0,1)$ there is a real $r\\geq0$ with $D^{\\mathrm{reg}}(\\rho\\Vert F)=r$, $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}b_n^\\epsilon=r$ and $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}u_n=r$. The value $r$ does not depend on $\\epsilon$."}, "numbered_source": true}
