Metadata: {"id": "B02", "fqn": "GourResource.gsl_b02", "source": {"gour": {"ref": "Thm 11.4 (where the foundation is used; no separate number)", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.05474v2#nameddest=theorem.11.4", "page": 504}, "hy": [{"ref": "Lemma 3", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2408.02722v3#Thmtheorem3"}]}, "statement": "For pairs of density operators of the standard model, gives the correspondence of $\\beta$, the hypothesis-testing relative entropy and effect operators, and their transport by isometric isomorphisms and quantum channels. It includes the existence of optimal tests and of tests accepting with equality for $\\epsilon\\in[0,1)$, monotonicity in the error, the value $-\\log_2(1-\\epsilon)$ on self-pairs, and the data-processing inequality for quantum channels.", "book": "Correspondence with finite-dimensional models: the hypothesis-testing quantities β, dh and optimal tests [foundation; used in the proof of the main theorem; no separate theorem number]", "original": {"gour": "\\begin{myt}{\\color{yellow} The Generalized Quantum Stein's Lemma}\n\\begin{theorem}\\label{gsl}\nLet $\\mf$ be a QRT whose sets of free states satisfy the properties above.\nThen, for all $\\eps\\in(0,1)$ and all $\\rho\\in\\md(A)$\n\\be\\label{1158}\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{n}D_{\\min}^{\\eps}\\left(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\big\\|\\mf\\right)=D^{\\reg}(\\rho\\|\\mf)\\;.\n\\ee\n\\end{theorem}", "hy": "\\begin{align}\n    \\mathcal{T}_{\\epsilon,\\rho}\\coloneqq\\qty{T:0\\leq T\\leq \\mathds{1}, \\Tr[(\\mathds{1}-T)\\rho]\\le \\epsilon},\n\\end{align}\nand $\\mathds{1}$ is the identity operator.\nTo study the generalized quantum Stein's lemma, we consider a more general setting where the hypotheses are given by the state $\\rho$ and a compact set $\\mathcal{S}$ of states on the system $\\mathcal{H}$, where the compactness is assumed to guarantee the existence of maxima and minima in the following analysis.\nIn this case, the performance is characterized by\n\\begin{align}\n&\\beta_\\epsilon\\left(\\rho\\middle\\| \\mathcal{S}\\right)\\coloneqq\\min_{T\\in\\mathcal{T}_{\\epsilon,\\rho}}\\max_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}} \n\\Tr[T \\sigma].\n\\end{align}\nSuch a hypothesis is called a composite hypothesis. \n\nGenerally, the quantities satisfy the max-min inequality~\\cite[Section~5.4.1]{boyd_vandenberghe_2004}\n\\begin{align}\n\\Label{BH4Y}\n\\max_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}_2}\n\\beta_{\\epsilon}\\left(\\rho\\middle\\| \\sigma\\right) \n&\\leq \\beta_{ \\epsilon}\\left(\\rho\\middle\\| \\mathcal{S}\\right).\n\\end{align}\nInterestingly, as shown in Section \\ref{S2},\nthese two quantities have the following relation when the set $\\mathcal{S}$ in the second argument is convex, due to the minimax theorem~\\cite{v1928theorie,sion1958general,10.2996/kmj/1138038812}.\n\n\\begin{lemma}\\label{L1}\nFor any $\\epsilon\\geq 0$, any state $\\rho$, and any convex compact set $\\mathcal{S}$, we have\n\\begin{align}\n\\max_{\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{S}}\n\\beta_{\\epsilon}\\left(\\rho\\middle\\| \\sigma\\right)\n= \\beta_{\\epsilon}\\left(\\rho\\middle\\| \\mathcal{S}\\right).\n\\Label{BH4}\n\\end{align}\n\\end{lemma}"}, "signature_sha256": "edf004cf02e8373efa795ecf71475cbc5ad69d2e7fbc6ae080b4ba3bfd30b224", "display": {"section": "foundation", "statement": "For pairs of density operators of the standard model, gives the correspondence of $\\beta$, the hypothesis-testing relative entropy and effect operators, and their transport by isometric isomorphisms and quantum channels. It includes the existence of optimal tests and of tests accepting with equality for $\\epsilon\\in[0,1)$, monotonicity in the error, the value $-\\log_2(1-\\epsilon)$ on self-pairs, and the data-processing inequality for quantum channels."}, "numbered_source": false}
